1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's the greatest invasion machine of World War II.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Thousands of ships and aircraft, millions of tons of steel and concrete,

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and tens of thousands of Allied soldiers dropping from the skies and storming the beaches of Normandy.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:23,000
They overcame the Atlantic wall and nature itself in the greatest assault the world had ever seen.

5
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
The hardware that triumphed on the longest day.

6
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Machines of D-Day next on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,280
12.16 a.m. June 6, 1944. D-Day.

8
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:50,880
Four years of trial and error have trained the men

9
00:00:50,881 --> 00:00:52,420
and built the war machine

10
00:00:52,421 --> 00:00:55,300
that would throw themselves against Hitler's Atlantic Wall.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Ever since the Blitzkrieg of 1940, Europe was under the iron heel of Nazi jackboots.

12
00:01:03,001 --> 00:01:07,000
Now the operation that would drive them out was in motion.

13
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:12,000
Each element of this invasion machine went through intensive research and development

14
00:01:12,001 --> 00:01:15,000
to make sure it would perform its job perfectly.

15
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:20,000
The first unit to spring into action was the Allied Airborne Force.

16
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:25,000
Highly trained paratroopers prepared to leap from their aircraft into the night sky over Normandy.

17
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:26,000
over Normandy.

18
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:36,720
So we know exactly what we were doing and to stand up, hook up and then check equipment

19
00:01:36,721 --> 00:01:45,520
and would check the equipment of the man in front of me and check my own equipment.

20
00:01:45,521 --> 00:01:47,920
The idea was to get out of that plane.

21
00:01:47,921 --> 00:01:50,240
We were like, what's it called, an airborne shuffle.

22
00:01:50,241 --> 00:01:54,980
You had your knee and the next guy's behind and you were shuffling out so that when you

23
00:01:54,981 --> 00:01:56,980
you were close together.

24
00:01:56,981 --> 00:02:02,980
Carrying the first wave of paratroopers on D-Day was the C-47 transport aircraft.

25
00:02:02,981 --> 00:02:08,980
Well, outside of the Model T Ford, I think the C-47 was one of the great inventions of the millennium.

26
00:02:08,981 --> 00:02:12,980
The man behind this incredible aircraft was Donald Douglas.

27
00:02:12,981 --> 00:02:19,980
Asked by TWA to develop a passenger airliner to rival Boeing's models, his design was superb.

28
00:02:19,981 --> 00:02:29,080
Streamlined and wide-bodied, the fully developed aircraft took to the skies over America in 1935 as the DC-3 passenger airliner.

29
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:33,032
The aircraft revolutionized air transport.

30
00:02:33,033 --> 00:02:39,774
Fast and powerful with an outstanding safety record, the DC-3 soon came to the attention of the U.S.

31
00:02:39,775 --> 00:02:45,100
Army Air Force and entered service only 16 days after Pearl Harbor as the C-47.

32
00:02:45,101 --> 00:02:52,040
It was the ideal military transport aircraft and served all over the world.

33
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:56,820
Now, it was crucial to the airborne troops in the early hours of D-Day.

34
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:06,740
It was the responsibility of the crew of the C-47 to make certain that the paratroopers

35
00:03:06,741 --> 00:03:13,160
were dropped as close to target as possible, and even a delay of three or four seconds

36
00:03:13,161 --> 00:03:20,160
and turning on the green light would have thrown them off their designated target zone.

37
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:44,180
Out we went.

38
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:47,980
And the war's on.

39
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:04,860
On D-Day, more than 1,000 C-47s carried 23,000 airborne troops into action.

40
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:12,420
The ideally placed door in the C-47 meant that a full load of 20 paratroopers could leave the aircraft in just 10 seconds.

41
00:04:12,421 --> 00:04:17,420
The cargo carrying ability of the aircraft was also improved when this door was enlarged.

42
00:04:17,421 --> 00:04:22,420
This meant that heavy equipment and even wounded men on stretchers could easily be loaded aboard.

43
00:04:22,421 --> 00:04:29,420
We would haul litter patients, gasoline, ammunition, food, medical supplies.

44
00:04:29,421 --> 00:04:36,420
Anything that had to be hauled to get to where it was destined to go in a hurry, the C-47 did it.

45
00:04:36,421 --> 00:04:42,980
It was so dependable for so many jobs. It was just a flying truck.

46
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:49,680
Everything depended on whether or not the paratroopers could protect the flanks of the landing beaches

47
00:04:49,681 --> 00:04:52,180
and capture the routes needed for the advance inland.

48
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:58,000
But the paratroopers could only carry light equipment and were vulnerable to armored attack.

49
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:03,580
The Allies needed to find a way to get artillery and anti-tank guns right onto the drop zones.

50
00:05:03,581 --> 00:05:06,100
The answer to this problem was the glider.

51
00:05:06,420 --> 00:05:10,400
Two types of gliders were used by the Allied Airborne Divisions on D-Day.

52
00:05:10,401 --> 00:05:18,660
The British Horsa was 67 feet long, built almost entirely of wood, and carried 25 troops or a 6-pound anti-tank gun.

53
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:27,320
The smaller American CG-4 Waco had a fabric and plywood fuselage over a metal framework and could carry a jeep or 15 men.

54
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:34,920
The cheap, lightweight construction of both designs meant that they could easily be towed by the C-47.

55
00:05:34,921 --> 00:05:39,300
If necessary, two Wacos could even be towed at the same time.

56
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:46,860
The pinpoint accuracy of these simple throwaway aircraft also made them ideal for special missions on D-Day.

57
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:51,700
As U.S. paratroopers hit the ground in the American sector,

58
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:56,480
50 miles away, two gliders carrying men of the British 6th Airborne Division,

59
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:00,560
led by Major John Howard, were about to show what gliders could do.

60
00:06:00,561 --> 00:06:06,300
In what has been described as one of the greatest flying achievements of the war,

61
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:09,840
they located the vital Ornn Canal Bridge in the pitch dark

62
00:06:09,841 --> 00:06:12,980
and swooped in to crash land at 100 miles an hour.

63
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:15,780
Then there was a great scramble to get out

64
00:06:15,781 --> 00:06:17,500
because, you know, the last thing you want to be

65
00:06:17,501 --> 00:06:20,660
when you just landed close to a guarded bridge

66
00:06:20,661 --> 00:06:23,360
is to be sitting in a wooden glider.

67
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:27,360
Almost as soon as we cleared the glider,

68
00:06:27,361 --> 00:06:31,580
The major hard typical of him, he was up on the bank of the canal saying,

69
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:35,540
come on boys, this is it, come on, and we'll be all charged after him.

70
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:40,200
The German defenders were totally taken by surprise.

71
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:43,280
By the time we got to that end of the bridge,

72
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:46,700
they decided it was not a good place to be staying around.

73
00:06:47,540 --> 00:06:51,140
We scattered either side of the bridge up and down the canal bank

74
00:06:51,141 --> 00:06:54,020
and took up all our defensive positions.

75
00:06:54,021 --> 00:07:02,220
As this actual photo taken on D-Day shows, the capture of the Orn Canal Bridge blocked the German route to the D-Day invasion beaches.

76
00:07:04,260 --> 00:07:11,320
The men of the glider force were the vanguard of 150,000 troops who landed in Normandy over the next few hours.

77
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:17,520
But after this promising start, things began to go badly for the airborne invasion.

78
00:07:17,521 --> 00:07:26,360
Cloud banks over the drop zones caused the C-47 transports to veer off course, and heavy anti-aircraft fire broke up their formations.

79
00:07:29,480 --> 00:07:38,440
The paratroopers of the American Airborne Divisions were scattered all over the Cotantan Peninsula, while vital heavy equipment was lost in swamps and rivers.

80
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:42,840
Over the next few hours, small groups of men began to band together.

81
00:07:42,841 --> 00:07:47,360
Dick Winters was a young officer of the 101st Airborne

82
00:07:47,361 --> 00:07:49,900
and one of the first men on the ground on D-Day.

83
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:57,560
He worked with another paratrooper to determine exactly where they were.

84
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:02,900
We exchanged greetings, and in coming down the road,

85
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:07,520
he had been smart enough to stop at a stone marker.

86
00:08:08,460 --> 00:08:11,060
The stone marker said St. Muir Eglise.

87
00:08:11,061 --> 00:08:19,480
I was able to orient the map very quickly because the planes were coming in and they were flying from west to east.

88
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:23,540
He had told me St. Mary Glees is that town up there.

89
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:28,280
There was St. Mary Glees in my map. I knew exactly where it was.

90
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:34,140
Winters and his men then began to secure the vital road exits from Utah Beach.

91
00:08:34,141 --> 00:08:42,240
By 3.45 a.m., a huge fleet of C-47s had taken the airborne divisions to Normandy.

92
00:08:42,920 --> 00:08:53,980
1,300 aircraft and 800 gliders had carried 23,000 men, 110 jeeps, and 500 artillery pieces to drop zones deep behind enemy lines.

93
00:08:54,720 --> 00:09:03,020
The C-47s were now the paratroopers' lifeline, bringing in vital supplies and reinforcements until they could link up with the troops landing on the beaches.

94
00:09:03,021 --> 00:09:05,840
But the day had only just started.

95
00:09:06,300 --> 00:09:09,100
More men and more machines were about to roll into action.

96
00:09:10,120 --> 00:09:15,180
All of your heroic airborne guys dropping on the Cotentin Peninsula

97
00:09:15,181 --> 00:09:18,320
and heroically walking through the night,

98
00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:24,000
they are only part of a very big, complicated system.

99
00:09:24,840 --> 00:09:28,600
Unless every single man and machine did their job to perfection,

100
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:31,280
D-Day would end in chaos.

101
00:09:33,020 --> 00:09:38,020
The idea of airborne forces dates back to Benjamin Franklin.

102
00:09:38,021 --> 00:09:43,020
He envisioned a time when soldiers would be delivered from the sky using contraptions resembling parachutes.

103
00:09:43,021 --> 00:09:46,020
Machines of D-Day will return in a moment.

104
00:09:54,020 --> 00:09:58,020
We now return to Modern Marvels.

105
00:09:58,021 --> 00:09:58,721
At 6 a.m.

106
00:09:58,722 --> 00:10:09,140
on June 6, 1944, the largest invasion fleet the world had ever seen appeared in front of stunned German soldiers manning bunkers along the Normandy coast.

107
00:10:09,660 --> 00:10:15,320
60 miles of ocean packed with nearly 6,000 ships and craft of every shape and size.

108
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:22,380
When the landing fleet arrived, it was unbelievably huge.

109
00:10:23,240 --> 00:10:25,100
We said, it can't be real.

110
00:10:25,101 --> 00:10:29,440
so many ships and the horizon was black with ships.

111
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:34,040
We said we can do nothing now.

112
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:39,660
We were actually threading through in daylight.

113
00:10:40,660 --> 00:10:45,800
These hundreds and hundreds of vessels all in straight lines,

114
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:48,720
a number that you couldn't have imagined.

115
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:53,240
You'd look ahead and see these lines stretching right to the horizon.

116
00:10:55,100 --> 00:11:01,020
Seconds after appearing out of the mist, the invasion fleet announced its arrival with a terrifying bombardment.

117
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:11,920
Like clockwork, the battleships, cruisers, and destroyers each added their contribution to the weight of metal smashing down on German beach fortifications.

118
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:16,080
A storm of 15-inch, 8-inch, and 5-inch shells.

119
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:36,740
Joining these conventional fighting ships were new craft designed especially for this day, rocket ships.

120
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:42,740
Their decks were crammed with racks holding hundreds of rockets tipped with 5-inch warheads.

121
00:11:43,340 --> 00:11:47,420
A barrage from a single rocket ship could pulverize hundreds of yards of beach,

122
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:50,740
shredding barbed wire entanglements and obliterating minefields.

123
00:11:50,741 --> 00:11:58,600
under the cover of the bombardment assault troops prepared to cross the last miles of sea to the

124
00:11:58,601 --> 00:12:08,620
shore we climbed over into the landing craft and we were you know 10 11 miles out and the sea was

125
00:12:08,621 --> 00:12:15,040
rough and that's when everybody got wet and cold and you're getting seasick the machines that

126
00:12:15,041 --> 00:12:19,660
carried men like Bob slaughter to their destiny on the beaches of France spent years in the making

127
00:12:20,220 --> 00:12:26,240
Without them, an amphibious landing, one of the most complex and dangerous operations in war, would have been impossible.

128
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:33,000
Not only do you have to deal with the land, not only do you have to deal with the sea,

129
00:12:33,720 --> 00:12:37,260
but in amphibious operations, you have to deal with the interface.

130
00:12:37,261 --> 00:12:41,000
You've got to cross the line between the two.

131
00:12:43,120 --> 00:12:48,180
The first major amphibious landing in modern times dates back to World War I.

132
00:12:48,181 --> 00:12:58,800
In 1915, Britain used its naval power to bypass the deadlock of trench warfare on the Western Front in France with an assault on Turkey's Gallipoli Peninsula.

133
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:05,000
Thousands of men stormed the beaches to destroy the gun positions, blocking the route to the Black Sea and Russia.

134
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:07,160
The assault was a disaster.

135
00:13:08,940 --> 00:13:17,300
No one had thought through how you do these kind of operations, how you coordinate the naval gunfire, how you coordinate the naval landings.

136
00:13:17,301 --> 00:13:23,020
They didn't have the equipment, so you're essentially jerry-rigging equipment for the landings,

137
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:24,900
and none of the troops have been trained for it.

138
00:13:25,020 --> 00:13:29,340
The British Army, the Australians, the New Zealanders are all lined up on the beach,

139
00:13:29,460 --> 00:13:35,300
coming ashore in small boats and being shredded by Turkish machine guns and artillery.

140
00:13:36,020 --> 00:13:42,640
So the story of Gallipoli is kill, kill, kill, and it's the Turks doing all the killing.

141
00:13:42,641 --> 00:13:47,640
Gallipoli made a strong impression on military planners in America and Britain.

142
00:13:47,641 --> 00:13:54,640
Both countries saw the potential of amphibious warfare, if only technology could catch up.

143
00:13:54,641 --> 00:14:01,640
Up to the early 1930s, infantry still had to land on beaches by jumping out of boats and wading ashore.

144
00:14:01,641 --> 00:14:06,640
By World War II, Allied designers realized that a brand new machine was needed,

145
00:14:06,641 --> 00:14:11,640
a machine that could carry troops across that deadly divide between land and sea.

146
00:14:11,641 --> 00:14:20,240
They need boats that are designed to come into shallow water, come right up to the beach, and get the cargo off quickly.

147
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:30,160
And the Americans, among other places, turn to people whose job it is to design and build river craft.

148
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:32,708
In 1938, the U.S.

149
00:14:32,709 --> 00:14:38,960
Marines first tested a new shallow draft boat capable of landing troops and small vehicles.

150
00:14:38,961 --> 00:14:44,240
Made by a New Orleans-based Maverick boat builder, Andrew Jackson Higgins, it was an

151
00:14:44,241 --> 00:14:50,580
early version of the landing craft that became the backbone of the D-Day invasion.

152
00:14:50,581 --> 00:14:54,840
Britain too was developing specialized landing craft to carry troops and vehicles.

153
00:14:54,841 --> 00:14:59,720
But these early craft were slow and awkward, and before they could be fully developed,

154
00:14:59,721 --> 00:15:08,200
Europe was once again engulfed by war.

155
00:15:08,201 --> 00:15:15,200
In 1940, Britain's tiny force of landing craft was destroyed, not in landing, but in evacuating troops from Dunkirk.

156
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:24,200
Yet only a few weeks later, Winston Churchill ordered amphibious raids on occupied France to begin.

157
00:15:24,700 --> 00:15:29,700
A new organization was set up to coordinate these raids and to develop the machines they needed.

158
00:15:30,200 --> 00:15:36,700
Newly trained forces of commandos would carry out these missions in what would become known as combined operations.

159
00:15:36,701 --> 00:15:41,700
These early commando raids were mere pinpricks against the Nazi war machine,

160
00:15:41,701 --> 00:15:45,700
but were key to working out every possible problem before D-Day.

161
00:15:46,700 --> 00:15:47,818
Meanwhile, the U.S.

162
00:15:47,819 --> 00:15:52,700
pushed ahead with its own landing craft designs and amphibious invasion techniques.

163
00:15:58,700 --> 00:16:03,700
The basic landing craft that carried the assault divisions on D-Day was about 40 feet long,

164
00:16:03,701 --> 00:16:09,400
long, weighed 13 tons, and carried 30 infantrymen. The secret of the successful

165
00:16:09,401 --> 00:16:12,760
assault landing craft was the flat bottom, which stopped it from getting

166
00:16:12,761 --> 00:16:16,720
stuck on shore, and a ramp bow that allowed troops to disembark quickly

167
00:16:16,721 --> 00:16:22,480
before they became easy targets for enemy guns. In the United States, they

168
00:16:22,481 --> 00:16:28,840
were known as landing craft vehicle personnel, or LCVP. Britain used a similar

169
00:16:28,841 --> 00:16:33,400
design tested in early commando raids, called the landing craft assault, or LCA.

170
00:16:33,401 --> 00:16:37,400
The LCA was quite a good sea boat.

171
00:16:37,401 --> 00:16:45,400
It was low in the water and specifically designed to come in at night as a surprise attack.

172
00:16:45,401 --> 00:16:49,400
But they were wider than the LCVP.

173
00:16:49,401 --> 00:16:53,400
They were a little bit lower and they had more armor on them.

174
00:16:53,401 --> 00:16:57,400
It was probably a better landing craft than the LCVP.

175
00:16:57,401 --> 00:17:02,400
The LCVPs were made of plywood and these were steel.

176
00:17:02,401 --> 00:17:08,160
When they left their motherships on D-Day, the landing craft headed to the beaches in

177
00:17:08,161 --> 00:17:09,720
long columns.

178
00:17:09,721 --> 00:17:14,500
Over the last few hundred yards, they spread out to form a line or wave so that all their

179
00:17:14,501 --> 00:17:20,000
troops could hit the beach at exactly the same moment.

180
00:17:20,001 --> 00:17:24,460
To break up the wave formation, the Germans constructed thousands of obstacles along the

181
00:17:24,461 --> 00:17:26,120
beaches of France.

182
00:17:26,121 --> 00:17:30,280
These could rip the bottoms out of landing craft or blow them and their human cargo to

183
00:17:30,281 --> 00:17:33,280
pieces.

184
00:17:33,281 --> 00:17:40,220
At dawn on D-Day, the coxswains of 2,400 LCAs and LCVPs knew that they were heading straight

185
00:17:40,221 --> 00:17:42,220
for a killing zone.

186
00:17:42,221 --> 00:17:46,880
Every man had to use all his skill to avoid the obstacles and get his troops safely ashore

187
00:17:46,881 --> 00:17:52,180
right on target, or the first critical moments of the invasion would be a disaster.

188
00:17:52,181 --> 00:17:55,620
landing 18 LCAs of the first wave on Omaha Beach

189
00:17:55,621 --> 00:17:57,380
was Lieutenant Jimmy Green.

190
00:17:57,381 --> 00:17:59,580
We had the easiest wave, I think,

191
00:17:59,581 --> 00:18:02,360
because we were clear of the obstacles.

192
00:18:02,361 --> 00:18:04,960
The second and third waves landed actually

193
00:18:04,961 --> 00:18:06,060
in amongst the obstacles.

194
00:18:06,061 --> 00:18:09,500
Some of our craft were hit and disabled.

195
00:18:09,501 --> 00:18:12,300
Our 18 LCAs, we lost six.

196
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:20,180
We didn't know whether we were gonna land

197
00:18:20,181 --> 00:18:24,180
right in front of a German pillbox or a gun emplacement or what.

198
00:18:24,181 --> 00:18:28,180
And I remember telling the rest of them, I said, we're going to catch hell.

199
00:18:30,180 --> 00:18:36,180
As they went up the beach, the Germans opened up with their machine guns and wiped them out.

200
00:18:36,181 --> 00:18:40,180
I think there were something like two wounded survivors when the second wave went in.

201
00:18:40,181 --> 00:18:44,180
The terrible casualties did not slow down the pace of the invasion.

202
00:18:44,181 --> 00:18:50,180
Each successive wave reaching the beach added to the overwhelming buildup of troops.

203
00:18:50,181 --> 00:18:53,320
to hurl themselves against the German defenses.

204
00:18:53,321 --> 00:18:56,300
But this was only the very tip of the Allied spearhead,

205
00:18:56,301 --> 00:18:59,220
and it could still be blunted by a heavy attack on land,

206
00:18:59,221 --> 00:19:01,340
and above all, from the air.

207
00:19:01,341 --> 00:19:03,520
If the invasion was to be a success,

208
00:19:03,521 --> 00:19:06,860
the D-Day machine had to win control of the skies

209
00:19:06,861 --> 00:19:07,860
over Normandy.

210
00:19:12,160 --> 00:19:14,700
Along with the bulldozer and the 6x6 truck,

211
00:19:14,701 --> 00:19:16,300
General Dwight Eisenhower declared

212
00:19:16,301 --> 00:19:19,780
the C-47 transport aircraft and the landing craft

213
00:19:19,781 --> 00:19:24,740
As the four machines that won World War II, Machines of D-Day will return in a moment.

214
00:19:31,860 --> 00:19:34,780
We now return to Modern Marvels.

215
00:19:36,940 --> 00:19:39,360
Mid-morning June 6, 1944.

216
00:19:40,140 --> 00:19:43,180
Allied aircraft patrolled the skies high above the beaches

217
00:19:43,181 --> 00:19:46,360
where thousands of troops were pouring ashore from landing craft.

218
00:19:46,361 --> 00:19:50,360
This was the most vulnerable moment of the entire invasion.

219
00:19:50,361 --> 00:19:54,360
If the German Air Force attacked now, they could cause chaos on the beaches.

220
00:19:54,361 --> 00:19:59,360
Because an amphibious operation is so vulnerable to air attack,

221
00:19:59,361 --> 00:20:05,360
it is a necessary precondition to any amphibious operation

222
00:20:05,361 --> 00:20:09,360
that you have control of the skies overhead.

223
00:20:09,361 --> 00:20:13,360
Although fighter aircraft dominated the skies on D-Day,

224
00:20:13,361 --> 00:20:14,600
on D-Day.

225
00:20:14,601 --> 00:20:16,880
The story of the destruction of the Luftwaffe

226
00:20:16,881 --> 00:20:18,880
began months earlier with the heavy bombers

227
00:20:18,881 --> 00:20:21,660
of the US 8th Air Force.

228
00:20:21,661 --> 00:20:25,820
Their mission to destroy the Luftwaffe on the ground.

229
00:20:25,821 --> 00:20:32,300
Every target that we had was German aircraft factories.

230
00:20:32,301 --> 00:20:35,420
Either the engine factories or the plane themselves

231
00:20:35,421 --> 00:20:39,220
had something to do with the German Air Force.

232
00:20:39,221 --> 00:20:41,440
Though the bombers caused severe damage,

233
00:20:41,441 --> 00:20:43,840
German fighters made them pay a heavy price.

234
00:20:49,680 --> 00:20:51,900
Listen, I'm telling you, those fighters sometimes,

235
00:20:52,480 --> 00:20:54,320
it just looked like a swarm of bees.

236
00:20:54,500 --> 00:20:55,860
They would catch it in different spots.

237
00:20:56,560 --> 00:20:59,800
Until you got to the target, you were very fortunate

238
00:20:59,801 --> 00:21:01,680
if you got through it and didn't get something hit.

239
00:21:04,720 --> 00:21:08,460
To take on enemy aircraft that attacked bombers on their way to targets,

240
00:21:08,461 --> 00:21:12,360
a high-performance, long-range escort fighter was desperately needed.

241
00:21:13,380 --> 00:21:19,740
Rolls-Royce engineers tried fitting a low-level American fighter called the P-51 with their famous Merlin engine.

242
00:21:20,680 --> 00:21:25,860
The result was the best allied fighter of the war, and it would prove crucial to the D-Day invasion.

243
00:21:27,100 --> 00:21:32,800
The new and improved P-51 Mustang not only had extraordinary performance at high altitude,

244
00:21:32,801 --> 00:21:38,320
but fitted with extra fuel tanks had the range to escort heavy bombers to distant targets.

245
00:21:38,460 --> 00:21:44,920
Arriving in Britain at the end of 1943, the new Mustang squadrons were just in time to

246
00:21:44,921 --> 00:21:49,920
support the bombers led by 8th Air Force Commander General Doolittle in the run-up to D-Day.

247
00:21:49,921 --> 00:21:55,060
General Doolittle said, now is the time for us to go after the fighters.

248
00:21:55,061 --> 00:22:01,580
The bombers had done their job, they destroyed the main targets, and they say from now on

249
00:22:01,581 --> 00:22:02,700
we're gonna go get them.

250
00:22:02,701 --> 00:22:05,820
Of course, then it was dog-eat-dog.

251
00:22:05,940 --> 00:22:08,120
Everybody was going after every damn thing that moved.

252
00:22:09,080 --> 00:22:09,980
We always attacked.

253
00:22:11,640 --> 00:22:14,880
We always attacked, no matter how many.

254
00:22:15,860 --> 00:22:17,680
And it shortened the war.

255
00:22:19,100 --> 00:22:20,620
In the months leading up to D-Day,

256
00:22:20,920 --> 00:22:24,580
more than 2,000 German fighters were destroyed in grueling air battles,

257
00:22:24,920 --> 00:22:28,140
crippling the Luftwaffe's ability to control the air over Europe.

258
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:32,180
I didn't dislike the Germans.

259
00:22:32,181 --> 00:22:34,080
I hated the son of a bitches.

260
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:37,040
So that took care of a lot of my problems

261
00:22:37,041 --> 00:22:40,960
because if I thought I could get one,

262
00:22:41,080 --> 00:22:42,000
I'd do a certain number.

263
00:22:42,080 --> 00:22:43,640
If I figured I could get a thousand,

264
00:22:43,780 --> 00:22:45,280
I'd have done a hell of a lot more.

265
00:22:47,160 --> 00:22:48,880
With the Luftwaffe decimated,

266
00:22:49,160 --> 00:22:51,700
the next preemptive strike for the invasion could begin.

267
00:22:53,620 --> 00:22:57,580
A bombing campaign against the French railway and road system began

268
00:22:57,581 --> 00:23:01,520
so that German reinforcements wouldn't be able to reach the Normandy beaches.

269
00:23:01,521 --> 00:23:12,940
They had us knocking out bridges and communications and trains south of the invasion.

270
00:23:13,240 --> 00:23:21,500
Now, the idea there was to prevent the Germans from bringing the troops down south up around to fight our boys when our boys went in.

271
00:23:21,960 --> 00:23:23,380
And we stopped them pretty good.

272
00:23:24,500 --> 00:23:30,280
To back up the bombers, fighters like the Mustang were assigned the task of attacking the trains themselves.

273
00:23:31,520 --> 00:23:44,100
In a single day, 46 locomotives were destroyed and another 32 damaged.

274
00:23:47,200 --> 00:23:55,440
On D-Day, as the invasion fleet neared the shore, a squadron of marauder bombers came in at low level just ahead of the first wave.

275
00:23:55,441 --> 00:24:06,740
The day that we hit, we went up and dropped 500-pound bombs in front of the cliffs, not on the Germans.

276
00:24:07,800 --> 00:24:18,020
We dug foxholes with bombs to where when our troops came in, they could dive down in the hole and protect themselves.

277
00:24:18,021 --> 00:24:22,220
Beyond the beaches, 33 squadrons of fighters flew inland,

278
00:24:22,221 --> 00:24:26,720
attacking German airfields in paralyzing attempts to strike at the invasion fleet.

279
00:24:26,721 --> 00:24:32,820
We were authorized to go down on the deck.

280
00:24:32,821 --> 00:24:35,620
And then you have a lot of fun there.

281
00:24:35,621 --> 00:24:37,020
Anything that moves, you shoot it.

282
00:24:37,021 --> 00:24:43,820
So we used up our ammunition, most of it, before we come back home.

283
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:54,040
Without the work of the Mustang, the thousands of men who had fought their way onto the beaches

284
00:24:54,041 --> 00:24:59,940
from landing craft would have been sitting ducks for the Luftwaffe.

285
00:24:59,941 --> 00:25:06,520
By mid-morning, the aerial component of the invasion machine dominated the skies overhead.

286
00:25:06,521 --> 00:25:10,900
On this extraordinary day, with the waters off the Normandy coast full of targets, the

287
00:25:10,901 --> 00:25:17,080
best Goering's once mighty Luftwaffe could do was a single brief sortie by just two fighters.

288
00:25:17,081 --> 00:25:23,520
In contrast, 3,000 Allied aircraft flew more than 14,000 missions in support of the troops

289
00:25:23,521 --> 00:25:25,320
below them.

290
00:25:25,321 --> 00:25:29,200
Yet on the beaches, those troops were still under German artillery fire.

291
00:25:29,201 --> 00:25:33,640
More tanks, artillery and vital supplies were needed to establish a bridgehead.

292
00:25:33,641 --> 00:25:38,840
The next wave of machines, amphibious craft and tank landing ships, began to move to the

293
00:25:38,841 --> 00:25:40,500
front of the battle line.

294
00:25:40,501 --> 00:25:45,760
Without them, the D-Day machine would grind to a halt.

295
00:25:45,761 --> 00:25:51,360
The biggest and most successful airborne operation in history was the Rhine Crossing in 1945.

296
00:25:51,660 --> 00:25:58,760
Codenamed Operation Varsity, it involved 1,700 transport aircraft and marked the beginning of the end for Germany.

297
00:25:59,060 --> 00:26:01,560
Machines of D-Day will return in a moment.

298
00:26:05,360 --> 00:26:07,860
We now return to Modern Marvels.

299
00:26:07,861 --> 00:26:12,880
Midday, June 6, 1944.

300
00:26:13,580 --> 00:26:16,700
Allied assault troops have successfully landed on the beaches of Normandy.

301
00:26:17,180 --> 00:26:20,400
They now needed vital supplies to help them push further inland.

302
00:26:21,180 --> 00:26:23,160
Coming in just behind the assault waves

303
00:26:23,161 --> 00:26:26,360
was one of the strangest craft to appear on the Normandy beaches,

304
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:29,720
the Dukw, also known as the Duck.

305
00:26:30,540 --> 00:26:34,360
For driver Stanley Dobson, D-Day was not what he had expected.

306
00:26:34,361 --> 00:26:38,360
Nobody told you what it was going to be like when you got there.

307
00:26:38,361 --> 00:26:42,360
I remember looking over the sand dunes at the time of unloading the duck

308
00:26:42,361 --> 00:26:46,360
and I saw the infantry actually fighting to take a cottage.

309
00:26:48,360 --> 00:26:52,360
I heard this, what I thought was the sound of bees,

310
00:26:52,361 --> 00:26:56,360
and suddenly realised it was machine gun bullets coming,

311
00:26:56,361 --> 00:26:58,360
it wasn't over the top.

312
00:26:58,361 --> 00:27:04,820
I suddenly realized, oh, what am I doing here?

313
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:14,420
The amphibious duck was a revolutionary design and answered another vital Allied need for a vehicle that traveled on land as easily as it navigated at sea.

314
00:27:15,020 --> 00:27:15,994
In 1942, the U.S.

315
00:27:15,995 --> 00:27:24,240
Army realized that even with a successful landing craft to get troops ashore, there would be a delay getting supplies forward to the front line.

316
00:27:24,241 --> 00:27:30,240
what they needed was another machine that could carry those supplies from transport ships inland to supply dumps.

317
00:27:31,240 --> 00:27:35,240
Leading yacht designer Rod Stevens was chosen to design this new vehicle.

318
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:41,240
In just 38 days, he and his team transformed a standard Army truck into an amphibian

319
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:44,240
by adding a boat-shaped hull and a propeller.

320
00:27:45,240 --> 00:27:49,240
It was built for whatever you're going to use it for, whether it be land or sea.

321
00:27:49,241 --> 00:27:53,000
or sea, you didn't have to worry about where you're going.

322
00:27:53,100 --> 00:27:53,960
You know you'll get there.

323
00:27:56,740 --> 00:27:59,800
It's just simple engineering when you get down to it.

324
00:27:59,900 --> 00:28:01,980
It may seem complicated to some people,

325
00:28:02,140 --> 00:28:04,160
but in theory, most of it is simple.

326
00:28:05,080 --> 00:28:08,280
The Duck could unload equipment from the heavy transports at sea

327
00:28:08,281 --> 00:28:10,400
and carry it inland right to its target.

328
00:28:10,880 --> 00:28:13,700
On D-Day, the astonishing versatility of the Duck

329
00:28:13,701 --> 00:28:16,280
was vital to the success of the Allied landings.

330
00:28:16,281 --> 00:28:23,040
While the duck carried urgently needed supplies to the troops already in action, waiting offshore

331
00:28:23,041 --> 00:28:28,700
were larger transport craft carrying the thousands of tanks, guns and trucks that had been stockpiled

332
00:28:28,701 --> 00:28:29,800
in Britain for the invasion.

333
00:28:30,860 --> 00:28:34,880
They would make all the difference to the fight on the beaches, but they needed a completely

334
00:28:34,881 --> 00:28:37,080
different kind of craft to get them ashore.

335
00:28:37,980 --> 00:28:42,700
As early as July 1940, Winston Churchill sent a note to the Minister of Supply.

336
00:28:42,701 --> 00:28:49,700
What is being done about designing and planning vessels to transport tanks across the sea for a British attack on enemy countries?

337
00:28:49,701 --> 00:28:56,700
The response in November of the same year was the landing craft tank, the LCT.

338
00:28:56,701 --> 00:29:01,700
Early models could carry five tanks, but later versions held double that number.

339
00:29:01,701 --> 00:29:08,700
With a crew of 12 and a speed of 11 knots, they provided the heavy lift necessary to carry tanks right up onto the beach.

340
00:29:08,701 --> 00:29:15,700
But to carry both troops and tanks in larger numbers, the only solution was the much bigger

341
00:29:15,701 --> 00:29:18,540
landing ship tank, or LST.

342
00:29:18,541 --> 00:29:24,880
The vast majority of LSTs were built in the dockyards of America.

343
00:29:24,881 --> 00:29:32,180
These 4,000-ton ships had two decks with room for 20 Sherman tanks and 200 troops.

344
00:29:32,181 --> 00:29:36,640
Just as important, they could act as motherships for the assault landing craft, carrying them

345
00:29:36,641 --> 00:29:40,180
on the upper deck and lowering them into the water like lifeboats.

346
00:29:40,181 --> 00:29:46,780
And like the smaller landing craft, the LSTs had flat bottoms with a ramp and bow doors.

347
00:29:46,781 --> 00:29:50,900
They could land right on the beach to unload their cargo.

348
00:29:50,901 --> 00:29:55,720
The landing ship tank is so mind-bogglingly useful that everybody wants one.

349
00:29:55,721 --> 00:29:57,740
In fact, they want a few hundred of them.

350
00:29:57,741 --> 00:30:03,320
The LST is the most popular girl at the dance, and her dance card is full.

351
00:30:03,321 --> 00:30:06,733
worked around the clock to roll out more than a thousand LSTs.

352
00:30:06,734 --> 00:30:08,220
Though demand for them came

353
00:30:08,221 --> 00:30:13,492
from the Pacific as well as Europe, planners knew that they were essential for D-Day.

354
00:30:13,493 --> 00:30:14,193
Even

355
00:30:14,194 --> 00:30:17,260
the date the invasion was launched depended on these priceless vessels.

356
00:30:17,920 --> 00:30:24,100
The availability of amphibious landing ships dictated the tempo of the war in the European

357
00:30:24,101 --> 00:30:29,392
theater. The Americans wanted to go cross-channel in 1942.

358
00:30:29,393 --> 00:30:32,860
They were persuaded out of that simply

359
00:30:32,861 --> 00:30:36,440
because we didn't have the landing ramp to go across channel.

360
00:30:38,360 --> 00:30:42,180
Off the Normandy beaches, the LSTs lowered their bow ramps

361
00:30:42,181 --> 00:30:45,680
to allow ducks to drive straight onto the cargo decks for more supplies.

362
00:30:46,540 --> 00:30:51,020
In a few hours, the LSTs themselves would be able to land on the beaches to unload.

363
00:30:51,620 --> 00:30:56,300
But this was a slow process, and the beached LSTs were easy targets for gunfire.

364
00:30:56,820 --> 00:31:01,180
The only way to speed up the unloading process was to have a fully functional port.

365
00:31:01,181 --> 00:31:08,020
Knowing that a port was essential to an invasion of France, British combined operations planners

366
00:31:08,021 --> 00:31:12,980
executed a large-scale commando raid to test defenses around the French port of Dieppe

367
00:31:12,981 --> 00:31:13,980
two years earlier.

368
00:31:13,981 --> 00:31:19,480
Now we ran into a convoy about half an hour or so before we were supposed to land.

369
00:31:19,481 --> 00:31:23,980
The port was then alert, was well defended, and all hell was then loose.

370
00:31:31,180 --> 00:31:36,920
The tanks put ashore.

371
00:31:37,080 --> 00:31:40,780
They weren't able to get off the beach because they didn't have any grip.

372
00:31:41,160 --> 00:31:42,560
And it was a complete disaster.

373
00:31:43,560 --> 00:31:46,540
It's something which I'd rather forget.

374
00:31:47,260 --> 00:31:51,040
But it did teach us a lesson not to attack a port.

375
00:31:51,640 --> 00:31:53,840
The tragedy of Dieppe was a major setback.

376
00:31:54,200 --> 00:31:59,120
Out of a raiding force of 6,000 men, more than 3,000 were killed, wounded, or captured.

377
00:31:59,121 --> 00:32:04,120
The Germans were now alert and expecting the Allies to attack a port as part of any invasion.

378
00:32:04,121 --> 00:32:10,120
Allied engineers had to find a solution to the port problem, or the D-Day invasion would be impossible.

379
00:32:10,121 --> 00:32:18,120
You can't get large quantities of supplies ashore from transport ships onto a beach.

380
00:32:18,121 --> 00:32:19,120
to a beach.

381
00:32:19,121 --> 00:32:24,420
So if you are not sure of getting or holding an existing

382
00:32:24,421 --> 00:32:30,720
port in working condition, you've got to bring your own.

383
00:32:30,721 --> 00:32:33,460
Taking a port to France was such a radical solution

384
00:32:33,461 --> 00:32:37,600
to the problem that many believed it was pure fantasy.

385
00:32:37,601 --> 00:32:40,440
Mervyn Walter was the young brigadier chosen to turn

386
00:32:40,441 --> 00:32:42,360
fantasy into reality.

387
00:32:42,361 --> 00:32:47,280
His ultra secret project would go down in history as Mulberry.

388
00:32:47,281 --> 00:32:50,280
to design the pieces for two great harbors,

389
00:32:51,520 --> 00:32:54,000
to construct the pieces for the two harbors

390
00:32:54,001 --> 00:32:55,740
all over the United Kingdom,

391
00:32:56,780 --> 00:33:00,600
to assemble the pieces on the English South Coast,

392
00:33:00,601 --> 00:33:03,120
to tow the pieces one by one

393
00:33:03,121 --> 00:33:07,320
through a hundred miles of German-infested sea

394
00:33:08,880 --> 00:33:10,980
to the French coast.

395
00:33:10,981 --> 00:33:14,820
That was the operation which was codenamed Mulberry.

396
00:33:14,821 --> 00:33:21,380
Each Mulberry Harbor had to enclose an area as large as New York's Central Park and handle

397
00:33:21,381 --> 00:33:25,580
as much shipping as a major port.

398
00:33:25,581 --> 00:33:30,040
Within the harbor walls were pier heads, which were essential features of the Mulberry.

399
00:33:30,041 --> 00:33:34,400
This was where the LSTs would discharge the cargo that would be the deciding factor in

400
00:33:34,401 --> 00:33:38,060
the success of D-Day.

401
00:33:38,061 --> 00:33:42,280
Mounted on legs that extended to the seabed, the platform between the legs was raised or

402
00:33:42,281 --> 00:33:43,926
lowered with the tide.

403
00:33:43,927 --> 00:33:48,640
From the pier heads to the shore were steel roadways, each span

404
00:33:48,641 --> 00:33:52,820
connected to the next with flexible joints that allowed them to follow the movement of

405
00:33:52,821 --> 00:33:58,189
the waves. Altogether, some 10 miles of floating roadway would be needed.

406
00:33:58,190 --> 00:33:59,880
To create the sheltered

407
00:33:59,881 --> 00:34:05,480
water for a harbor, breakwaters were built out of 147 enormous concrete boxes called

408
00:34:05,481 --> 00:34:06,512
Phoenix caissons.

409
00:34:06,513 --> 00:34:10,580
Able seaman Ken Bongard was amazed by his first sight of a caisson.

410
00:34:10,581 --> 00:34:22,220
The first thing I saw was a huge office block with no windows, no doors, standing by the

411
00:34:22,221 --> 00:34:23,004
quayside.

412
00:34:23,005 --> 00:34:30,320
In fact, it was 60 foot high, 60 foot wide and 200 feet long and looked like a giant

413
00:34:30,321 --> 00:34:36,190
dead box. It did not occur to us at that time that this thing floated.

414
00:34:36,191 --> 00:34:37,700
And we were amazed

415
00:34:37,701 --> 00:34:43,738
that the top it was tied up to us suddenly started pulling and we moved.

416
00:34:43,739 --> 00:34:45,080
More than 22,000

417
00:34:45,081 --> 00:34:47,546
men worked around the clock on the caissons.

418
00:34:47,547 --> 00:34:49,900
Astonishingly, they were completed in only

419
00:34:49,901 --> 00:34:54,080
nine months. Mervyn Walter's work was at the heart of the D-Day invasion.

420
00:34:54,081 --> 00:34:59,000
The British chiefs of staff wrote this,

421
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:08,920
The Mulberry project is so vital that it must be considered the crux of the whole operation, and it must not fail.

422
00:35:10,120 --> 00:35:16,760
By early afternoon on the Normandy beaches, the LSTs and docks were working desperately to get supplies ashore.

423
00:35:17,840 --> 00:35:21,600
Now the prefabricated Mulberry harbors took center stage.

424
00:35:22,260 --> 00:35:24,200
Without a steady flow of heavy equipment,

425
00:35:24,620 --> 00:35:28,020
the men who had fought and died on the beaches would have fought in vain.

426
00:35:28,660 --> 00:35:34,740
But would this huge revolutionary apparatus stand up to the pounding waves off the Normandy coast?

427
00:35:37,900 --> 00:35:43,640
Despite their nickname, Large Slow Target, LSTs suffered few losses during World War II.

428
00:35:44,160 --> 00:35:49,760
Only 26 were lost due to enemy action, and 13 were victims of weather, reef, or accident.

429
00:35:49,761 --> 00:35:52,620
machines of D-Day will return in a moment.

430
00:35:57,720 --> 00:36:00,740
We now return to Modern Marvels.

431
00:36:03,200 --> 00:36:05,060
2.30 p.m. on D-Day.

432
00:36:05,500 --> 00:36:08,260
The Mulberry Fleet, the anchor of the invasion machine,

433
00:36:08,600 --> 00:36:10,200
churned toward the Normandy coast.

434
00:36:10,840 --> 00:36:13,340
Engineers were already ashore marking out positions

435
00:36:13,341 --> 00:36:14,980
for the harbor's massive components.

436
00:36:14,981 --> 00:36:24,760
First to arrive were 63 obsolete battleships and cruisers and worn-out merchant ships making their final voyages to France.

437
00:36:25,440 --> 00:36:29,880
After being towed into position, they were sunk to form breakwaters off the landing beaches.

438
00:36:31,400 --> 00:36:37,620
At the same time, the crews of the Phoenix caissons were crossing the channel on their lumbering concrete vessels.

439
00:36:38,500 --> 00:36:44,660
There's one thing I've never forgotten to this day, and that was, of course, the sound of the waves.

440
00:36:44,980 --> 00:36:48,480
banging against the side of these caissons.

441
00:36:49,480 --> 00:36:50,980
It was like a huge drum.

442
00:36:50,981 --> 00:36:54,480
There was a huge boom, boom, boom going on all the time.

443
00:37:01,480 --> 00:37:06,980
Once into position, the crew aboard each caisson opened the valves that allowed the sea to rush in.

444
00:37:07,480 --> 00:37:11,480
Thousands of tons of concrete settled on the seabed in carefully marked sites

445
00:37:11,481 --> 00:37:13,980
to form the outlines of the two harbors.

446
00:37:13,981 --> 00:37:19,420
Finally, looming over the horizon came the strange shapes of the pierheads, ready to

447
00:37:19,421 --> 00:37:23,480
put down their legs and form the mooring point for the floating roadways.

448
00:37:23,481 --> 00:37:28,780
At these pierheads, the transport ships unloaded their troops and supplies directly onto trucks.

449
00:37:28,781 --> 00:37:33,220
They then took them along the roadways and across the beaches to supply areas inland.

450
00:37:33,221 --> 00:37:39,840
The naval construction battalions at Omaha Beach, the Seabees, and the Royal Engineers

451
00:37:39,841 --> 00:37:43,740
at Aeromage on Gold Beach worked day and night to get the harbors ready.

452
00:37:44,480 --> 00:37:48,460
The harbor itself was about the size of Dover Harbor,

453
00:37:49,260 --> 00:37:53,540
and considering how long it took to build it, it was quite a remarkable feat.

454
00:37:55,080 --> 00:37:59,240
In an incredibly short time, both Mulberry Harbors were completed.

455
00:37:59,900 --> 00:38:06,000
The heavy supplies, tanks, trucks, bridging and airfield construction equipment all started to dock.

456
00:38:06,001 --> 00:38:10,080
most of this heavy equipment was unloaded at the vital LST piers.

457
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:13,520
The landing ships could come on to the end of the roadway.

458
00:38:13,860 --> 00:38:16,140
The doors opened and the tanks just ran ashore.

459
00:38:16,480 --> 00:38:18,280
And they ran ashore from two levels.

460
00:38:18,760 --> 00:38:23,020
And we were able to discharge a complete landing ship tank in 40 minutes,

461
00:38:23,480 --> 00:38:25,820
whereas it took six hours on the beaches to.

462
00:38:26,120 --> 00:38:29,220
That was the difference between a port and the beach.

463
00:38:29,221 --> 00:38:38,820
Within the sheltered waters of the two harbors, hundreds of landing craft could beach.

464
00:38:38,821 --> 00:38:43,720
Simultaneously, hundreds of ducks scurried back and forth, keeping up a constant traffic

465
00:38:43,721 --> 00:38:48,120
of smaller loads to and from larger merchant vessels offshore.

466
00:38:48,121 --> 00:38:51,600
It seemed as though the Mulberry project was working perfectly.

467
00:38:51,601 --> 00:38:59,020
But then, disaster struck.

468
00:38:59,021 --> 00:39:06,880
On June 19th, one of the worst gales in memory threw up huge waves against the prefabricated harbors and shipping mast offshore.

469
00:39:10,740 --> 00:39:18,520
When the gale came, we had something like 200 craft moored inside the harbor, taking refuge.

470
00:39:19,020 --> 00:39:23,440
Every so often, one of them drew its anchor and started to wander.

471
00:39:23,441 --> 00:39:32,440
They would be taken in charge by a tug and prevented from rolling onto the long floating roadways which were so vulnerable.

472
00:39:32,441 --> 00:39:38,440
And he even had to sink two ships to prevent them from going on the road.

473
00:39:38,441 --> 00:39:44,440
With supplies slowing to a trickle, the advance inland had to be suspended while the storm pounded on.

474
00:39:44,441 --> 00:39:48,440
It seemed as though the overlord invasion machine was stopped in its tracks.

475
00:39:48,441 --> 00:39:52,360
tracks.

476
00:39:52,361 --> 00:39:58,180
As the seas finally subsided, the damage to the Mulberry Harbor at Omaha Beach was immense.

477
00:39:58,181 --> 00:40:03,300
The concrete caissons crumbled under the pounding of the waves, and even worse, ships disabled

478
00:40:03,301 --> 00:40:07,680
by the storm were driven into the harbor like floating battering rams.

479
00:40:07,681 --> 00:40:11,800
Whole sections of floating roadway were hurled onto the beach, along with the wreckage of

480
00:40:11,801 --> 00:40:15,240
landing craft and small ships.

481
00:40:15,241 --> 00:40:21,480
In the midst of the destruction, the ducks rose to the challenge.

482
00:40:21,481 --> 00:40:25,600
They worked their way around the wreckage, drove out to the ships whether they were beached

483
00:40:25,601 --> 00:40:34,340
or floating, and then went inland to unload right where supplies were needed.

484
00:40:34,341 --> 00:40:39,740
In no time at all, the remains of the Mulberry A at Omaha were salvaged to repair the almost

485
00:40:39,741 --> 00:40:43,240
intact Mulberry B at Arrow Marsh.

486
00:40:43,241 --> 00:40:47,820
started to flow again and the vital heavy equipment used the mulberry while the beaches

487
00:40:47,821 --> 00:40:52,574
handled the increasing flow of troops.

488
00:40:52,575 --> 00:40:59,080
Savage fighting held back the enemy while the supply

489
00:40:59,081 --> 00:41:00,232
buildup went on.

490
00:41:00,233 --> 00:41:05,920
C-47 transport aircraft continued to provide the armies with supplies and acted

491
00:41:05,921 --> 00:41:10,124
as flying ambulances to get the wounded back to hospitals in Britain.

492
00:41:10,125 --> 00:41:11,160
At the same time,

493
00:41:11,161 --> 00:41:14,160
Squadrons of Allied fighter bombers dominated the battlefield.

494
00:41:18,520 --> 00:41:22,820
The Allied armies, tanks, infantry, and massed artillery

495
00:41:22,821 --> 00:41:25,660
drove forward to liberate town after town.

496
00:41:29,780 --> 00:41:33,540
Two and a half million troops, half a million vehicles,

497
00:41:33,860 --> 00:41:36,920
and four million tons of supplies were delivered to France.

498
00:41:37,420 --> 00:41:39,900
It was an achievement unparalleled in history.

499
00:41:39,901 --> 00:41:43,900
Two words sum up the D-Day machine's performance.

500
00:41:43,901 --> 00:41:45,900
Mission accomplished.

501
00:41:45,901 --> 00:41:49,900
You know what the great hero of Normandy was?

502
00:41:49,901 --> 00:41:51,900
The one thing that made it all work?

503
00:41:51,901 --> 00:41:53,900
There isn't one.

504
00:41:53,901 --> 00:41:59,900
Normandy is an example of a system of systems.

505
00:41:59,901 --> 00:42:04,900
It is an example of many little moving parts

506
00:42:04,901 --> 00:42:11,900
parts brought into a single system through careful coordination.

507
00:42:11,901 --> 00:42:20,540
The level of coordination in both time and space was the largest effort ever undertaken

508
00:42:20,541 --> 00:42:22,540
by mankind.

509
00:42:22,541 --> 00:42:30,260
However small our part was, a very small cog in a very large machine, afterwards we began

510
00:42:30,261 --> 00:42:33,380
to feel very proud of what we'd done.

511
00:42:33,381 --> 00:42:39,620
The landing craft they developed and the cooperation that existed between the three services came

512
00:42:39,621 --> 00:42:41,920
to fruition on D-Day.

513
00:42:41,921 --> 00:42:46,140
Well, I think as far as the Duke's concerned, if they hadn't had the Duke, we wouldn't have

514
00:42:46,141 --> 00:42:47,140
had an Ormandy.

515
00:42:47,141 --> 00:42:50,340
It's as simple as that.

516
00:42:50,341 --> 00:42:56,140
These pilots and crew of the C-47 would go about anywhere and do anything.

517
00:42:56,141 --> 00:42:59,300
They were just wonderful people and very courageous.

518
00:42:59,301 --> 00:43:03,980
We all knew our job and we performed as best we could.

519
00:43:03,981 --> 00:43:09,140
Every crew member on every aircraft in our outfit did a tremendous job.

520
00:43:09,141 --> 00:43:12,900
The missions with the P-51 were more or less cut and dry.

521
00:43:12,901 --> 00:43:18,540
It's probably a horrible thing to say, but I got a hell of a kick out of it, particularly

522
00:43:18,541 --> 00:43:21,540
when you're winning.

523
00:43:21,541 --> 00:43:27,780
Ingenuity, imagination, and sheer hard work by dedicated teams had created one of the most

524
00:43:27,781 --> 00:43:31,220
complex and sophisticated war machines in history.

525
00:43:32,660 --> 00:43:36,260
The D-Day machine, a machine of men and metal,

526
00:43:36,261 --> 00:43:38,160
pushed the boundaries of engineering

527
00:43:38,161 --> 00:43:41,900
and defined freedom over 60 years ago.

528
00:43:45,940 --> 00:43:48,640
It was truly an unidentified flying object.

